scenario:
The travel agency Blue Lagoon is planning a Saturday night shutdown of the application server APP-01 to replace its disks. The operations director wants to know which business processes will be affected so that he can inform the branches and the call centre in good time. The architect works from a detailed model that contains these relationships:
– node “APP-01” –assignment→ artifact “booking.war” –realization→ application component “Booking Engine”;
– “Booking Engine” –assignment→ application function “Reservation Management” –realization→ application service “Create Reservation” –serving→ business process “Tour Sales”;
– “Booking Engine” –serving→ business process “Tour Complaint Handling”, which consists (composition) of the subprocesses “Complaint Intake” and “Compensation Payout”;
– business process “Manual Payment Verification” (the accounting department verifies payments for the application manually) –serving→ “Booking Engine”;
– node “APP-01” –association→ business process “Marketing Campaign”.
The director wants to distinguish what will be affected for certain and what only possibly, because he must inform the branches in writing about a certain outage.

The insurance company Atlas has a detailed model of claims handling with these relationships:
– business actor “Claims Department” –assignment→ business role “Claims Adjuster”;
– “Claims Adjuster” –assignment→ business process “Insurance Claim Settlement”;
– “Insurance Claim Settlement” –realization→ business service “Damage Resolution”;
– “Insurance Claim Settlement” –composition→ subprocess “Vehicle Inspection” (the process consists of several subprocesses);
– “Vehicle Inspection” –access (write)→ business object “Inspection Report”.
The division director is about to discuss a reorganisation of the department and wants a simplified view containing only the department, the service it offers, and the report the department works with. The architect does not want to draw anything in the view that does not follow from the detailed model, and therefore is to draw only relationships derived according to the ArchiMate 3.2 rules. The detailed model will be attached to the view as an appendix for the members of management.

The lending company Kredito has a detailed model of the loan provision process with these relationships:
– business process “Loan Application Processing” –composition→ subprocesses “Application Intake”, “Creditworthiness Assessment” and “Contract Signing”;
– business role “Credit Analyst” –assignment→ “Creditworthiness Assessment”;
– application component “ScoreX” –realization→ application service “Client Scoring”;
– “Client Scoring” –serving→ “Creditworthiness Assessment”.
Management is considering replacing the ScoreX system and wants a simplified Application Usage view only at the level of the whole process and roles. It asks which relationships of the “Client Scoring” service can be drawn in such a view so that the view claims nothing beyond the detailed model. The architect wants to justify every drawn relationship by the derivation rules of ArchiMate 3.2, because the view will be part of the materials for the tender for a new scoring system.

The pharmaceutical distributor FarmaLog is preparing materials for an audit of its supply chain. The detailed model contains these relationships:
– application component “Orders” –assignment→ application function “Order Processing”;
– component “Warehouse” –assignment→ function “Picking”;
– component “Transport” –assignment→ function “Delivery Planning”;
– “Order Processing” –flow→ “Picking” (passes on the confirmed order);
– “Picking” –flow→ “Delivery Planning” (passes on data about the parcels).
The integration architect wants an Application Cooperation view for the auditors containing only components, and asks which relationships between components can be drawn in it according to the derivation rules. The auditors will mainly ask whether data from orders (including patient data for made-to-order medicine preparation) also reaches the Transport application. The answer will be part of the audit report signed by the company’s managing director.

The district heating company Teplarny Sever is preparing for its CIO a simplified Layered view of the billing system in which application processes and the intermediate layer between technology and business objects are to be omitted. The detailed model contains these relationships:
– artifact “billing.jar” –realization→ application component “Invoicing”;
– “Invoicing” –assignment→ application process “Billing Calculation”;
– “Billing Calculation” –realization→ business process “Heat Billing” (the process is fully automated);
– “Billing Calculation” –access (read)→ data object “Meter Reading”;
– artifact “om_table” –realization→ data object “Delivery Point” –realization→ business object “Delivery Point”.
The architect is to draw into the simplified view derived relationships that preserve these connections and that are valid according to ArchiMate 3.2. The CIO will use the view in negotiations with the supplier of the new invoicing system, so it must not contain anything that does not follow from the model.

A junior architect at the online bookshop Listek drew, in a drawing tool that does not check the ArchiMate rules, a view of complaint handling support and asked for a review. The view contains these relationships:
(1) application component “CRM” –assignment→ business process “Complaint Handling” (an employee handles the complaint manually and uses the CRM for record-keeping);
(2) node “Server APP-02” –assignment→ application component “CRM” (the junior wanted to express where the CRM runs);
(3) business role “Complaints Officer” –assignment→ business actor “Customer Care Department”;
(4) data object “Complaint” –realization→ business object “Complaint”;
(5) business process “Complaint Handling” –realization→ business service “Customer Complaint Resolution”.
The CIO wants the view, after the review, to conform to ArchiMate 3.2 and the corrections to preserve the meaning the junior intended. The view will also serve as a model example for other juniors on the team.

The benefits agency is planning to replace the outdated application “Benefits Portal”, through which more than 400 thousand citizens submit an application every year. The model contains these relationships:
– application component “Benefits Portal” –assignment→ application process “Electronic Submission”;
– “Electronic Submission” –realization→ business process “Benefit Application Intake”;
– “Benefit Application Intake” –realization→ requirement “An application can be submitted online 24/7”;
– “An application can be submitted online 24/7” –realization→ goal “Simplify the citizen’s contact with the agency”;
– “An application can be submitted online 24/7” –influence (+)→ goal “Reduce application processing costs”.
The IT director wants to see in a Requirements Realization view which requirements and goals the portal contributes to and by what type of relationship, so that the new solution does not stop fulfilling them after the replacement. The view is to contain only relationships that validly follow from the model, because it will be an appendix to the tender documentation.

The company Cista Obec provides bulky waste collection for municipalities. A new analyst was given a business-layer view to study, which contains these relationships:
– business event “Collection order received” –triggering→ business process “Collection planning”;
– “Collection planning” –triggering→ AND junction and from it triggering leads to the processes “Container delivery” and “Advance invoice issuing”;
– “Collection planning” –flow→ business function “Vehicle fleet management”, the relationship is named “route plan”;
– between the process “Collection planning” and the business object “Collection plan” there is an access with an arrowhead at the object, between the process “Container delivery” and the same object an access with an arrowhead at the process;
– business collaboration “Collection crew” –aggregation→ business roles “Driver” and “Loader”; the role “Driver” is also aggregated by the business collaboration “Service call-out”.
The analyst is to summarise in her own words for the head of operations what the view says.

The online bookshop Ctenaruv Raj is preparing a strategy to increase online sales. An intern drew, in a general drawing tool that does not enforce the rules of the language, a motivation and strategy view and asked the architect to check it. The view contains these relationships:
(1) stakeholder “Sales Director” is connected by an association relationship to driver “Online sales share”;
(2) driver “Online sales share” –triggering→ goal “Increase e-shop conversion” (the intern wanted to say that the goal stems from the driver);
(3) goal “Increase e-shop conversion” –realization→ requirement “Three-click order” (the goal is to be fulfilled by this requirement);
(4) capability “Customer experience design” –realization→ course of action “Simplify the purchase journey”;
(5) capability “Customer experience design” –assignment→ resource “UX team” (the capability relies on the team);
(6) business process “Usability testing” –realization→ capability “Customer experience design”.
The architect is to determine which relationships contradict ArchiMate 3.2 and correct them so that the intended meaning is preserved.
